  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nA large low pressure system over the western United States is \nbringing cool conditions and mostly cloudy skies to the area \ntoday. While mostly dry conditions prevail, occasional \nprecipitation into Tuesday is forecast over southern Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming as waves of energy and moisture lift through the \narea. The first wave arrived late this morning into early this \nafternoon, bringing a period of light snow to areas around \nGardiner to Livingston, Montana. As this wave lifts north, the \nwave is drying out and precipitation is tapering off. The second \nwave, which is expected later today into Tuesday morning, is the \nremnants of Hurricane Rachel. Therefore, ample moisture exists \nwith this wave, and rain/snow showers are expected to be a bit \nmore widespread. However, precipitation may remain drizzle or \nflurries in many places, with only pockets of moderate rain or \nsnow. While colder air is in place over the region, cloud cover \nshould limit how much cooling exists tonight, so snow levels look \nto remain around 5,000 to 9,000 feet, lowest to the north. While \nthe chance remains low, pockets of freezing drizzle cannot be \nruled out around the Bighorn and Pryor Mountains tonight into \nTuesday morning, but warmer grounds should limit this threat. This\ncould (low chance) impact travel along Highway 14 through the \nBighorn Mountains. Otherwise, areas of fog are expected to develop\nacross the area tonight, with locally dense fog possible in spots\n(medium chance). \n\nTemperatures on Tuesday look to be about 10 degrees warmer than \ntoday as the influence from the low pressure system gradually \ndiminishes. With that said, occasional rain/snow showers may \nlinger into Tuesday afternoon and evening over/near the mountains \nsouth of Billings (30 percent chance). \n\nWednesday into Thursday look to be seasonable days with highs in \nthe 50s and 60s. Early Thursday, a dry backdoor cold front is \nexpected to bring breezy conditions to the area, with \nnorthwesterly wind gusts in the 20s (mph) for most. Locations over\nfar south-eastern Montana and northern Wyoming could see gusts \ninto the 30s and 40s mph as well. Ahead of the front, there is a \nslight chance of precipitation over far south-eastern Montana \nWednesday night (25 percent chance). \n\nFriday into Saturday will see temperatures warm back above normal\nas a ridge of high pressure moves into the region. Around \nLivingston to Nye, a favorable pressure gradient may set up \nSaturday morning, allowing breezy gap winds to develop. A mainly \ndry cold front on Sunday looks to end these winds, and bring \nslightly cooler temperatures to the area. LA",
